2. Project Overview
2.1 Research Background

Artificial intelligence technologies can now generate personalised video and audio content based on user preferences, text prompts, demographic information, previous interactions, or selected interests. Examples may include personalised educational videos, marketing messages, digital avatars, synthetic narration, customised advertisements, and AI-generated voice content.

Although these technologies may improve accessibility, efficiency, engagement, and user experience, they may also create ethical concerns. Relevant concerns include the collection of personal information, lack of informed consent, algorithmic bias, misleading synthetic media, unauthorised voice imitation, deepfakes, copyright issues, and uncertainty about whether users can identify AI-generated content.

Research into generative audio has identified ethical concerns including fraud, deepfakes, copyright infringement, privacy, bias, and the unauthorised use of human voice data. Responsible AI research therefore requires transparency about how AI is used, acknowledgement of limitations and bias, and clear identification of synthetic data or content.

This study will examine how users understand, experience, and evaluate AI-generated personalised video and audio content.

2.2 Research Objectives

The main objective of this research is to investigate user perceptions of AI-generated personalised video and audio content.

The specific objectives are:

To examine users’ awareness of AI-generated video and audio content.

To investigate whether personalisation increases user engagement and satisfaction.

To assess whether users trust video and audio content created by AI.

To examine users’ ability to distinguish between AI-generated and human-created content.

To identify user concerns relating to privacy, consent, authenticity, copyright, misinformation, voice cloning, and deepfakes.

To investigate whether clearly labelling content as AI-generated influences user trust and willingness to use the content.

To determine what safeguards users expect from organisations that create personalised AI video and audio content.

To develop recommendations for the responsible creation and use of personalised AI-generated media.

2.3 Research Questions

The research will address the following questions:

How aware are users of AI-generated personalised video and audio content?

How does personalisation affect users’ engagement with AI-generated content?

Do users trust AI-generated video and audio content?

Are users able to identify whether video or audio content has been generated by AI?

What privacy and ethical concerns do users have about personalised AI content?

Does clear disclosure that content was generated by AI affect user trust?

Are users comfortable with organisations using personal data to create customised video and audio content?

What safeguards should be introduced to reduce the risks associated with AI-generated personalised media?

2.4 Research Hypotheses

H1: Personalised AI-generated video and audio content has a positive relationship with user engagement.

H2: Participants are more likely to trust AI-generated content when it is clearly labelled as AI-generated.

H3: Participants with greater knowledge of artificial intelligence are more likely to identify AI-generated content.

H4: Privacy concerns have a negative relationship with users’ willingness to provide personal information for content personalisation.

H5: Users are less comfortable with personalised AI content that imitates a real person’s face or voice without explicit permission.

Null Hypothesis, H0: There is no significant relationship between AI-based personalisation and user engagement, satisfaction, or trust.

3. Methodology
3.1 Research Design

This research will use a mixed-methods, cross-sectional research design.

The quantitative component will use an anonymous online questionnaire to measure participant awareness, trust, engagement, privacy concerns, and attitudes towards AI-generated personalised video and audio content.

The qualitative component will include optional open-ended questions that allow participants to explain their concerns, experiences, and expectations in their own words.

The study will collect data at one point in time. It will investigate relationships between participants’ knowledge, attitudes, trust, and willingness to use personalised AI content. It will not claim that one factor directly causes another unless supported by appropriate evidence.

3.2 Target Participants

The target population will consist of adults who have experience using online video, audio, social media, digital advertising, educational platforms, streaming services, or generative AI tools.

Participants must:

Be at least 18 years old.
Be able to understand the Participant Information Statement.
Voluntarily agree to participate.
Have some experience consuming digital video or audio content.
Be able to complete an online questionnaire in English.

People under 18 will not be included because research involving minors would require additional safeguards and consent procedures.

3.3 Participant Recruitment

Participants will be recruited through approved communication channels, which may include:

Student communication groups.
Class announcements.
Email invitations.
Approved social media posts.
Online student communities.
Personal or professional networks where recruitment is permitted.

The recruitment invitation will explain:

The title and purpose of the study.
The eligibility requirements.
What participation involves.
The expected survey completion time.
That participation is voluntary.
That participants may stop before submitting the survey.
That participation will not affect their grades, employment, services, or relationship with the institution.
The researcher’s and supervisor’s contact details.

No lecturer, employer, manager, or group member will pressure an individual to participate. No financial incentive will be offered unless it has been approved by the institution.

3.4 Sample Size

The proposed sample size is approximately 50 to 100 adult participants.

This sample size is considered achievable for a student research project and should provide sufficient information for descriptive analysis and basic comparison between participant groups.

3.5 Data Collection Methods

Data will be collected using an anonymous online questionnaire. The questionnaire will contain approximately 20 to 25 questions and should take around 10 minutes to complete.

The questionnaire may include the following sections:

Section A: General information

Participants may be asked about:

Age group.
General level of education.
Frequency of internet use.
Frequency of watching online videos.
Frequency of listening to digital audio.
Previous use of generative AI tools.

Participants will not be asked for their full name, exact date of birth, residential address, telephone number, student number, employer, or personal account passwords.

Section B: Awareness of AI-generated content

Questions will examine whether participants:

Have previously seen AI-generated videos.
Have listened to AI-generated voices or music.
Understand the meaning of personalised AI content.
Believe they can identify AI-generated media.
Check whether online content is authentic.
Section C: User perceptions

Participants will rate statements such as:

Personalised AI videos are more engaging than general videos.
AI-generated audio can improve the accessibility of digital content.
I would use an AI-generated video designed for my interests.
I trust AI-generated content when its source is clearly disclosed.
AI-generated content should always be labelled.
I am concerned that AI video or audio may be used to mislead people.
I am uncomfortable with AI imitating a real person’s voice without permission.
I am willing to provide limited personal information for useful personalisation.

Responses may use a five-point Likert scale ranging from Strongly Disagree to Strongly Agree.

Section D: Optional content examples

Participants may be shown short examples of AI-generated or non-AI-generated video and audio content, provided that:

The examples are legally obtained or created for the research.
Copyright requirements are followed.
No real person’s face or voice is cloned without written permission.
No sensitive or distressing material is included.
The material is appropriate for an adult student research project.
Participants are informed when synthetic media is being used.
No attempt is made to deceive participants beyond an approved and clearly explained research procedure.

After viewing or listening, participants may be asked to rate:

Quality.
Relevance.
Personalisation.
Authenticity.
Trustworthiness.
Engagement.
Comfort level.
Willingness to use similar content.
Section E: Open-ended questions

Optional open-ended questions may include:

What benefits do you see in AI-generated personalised video and audio?

What concerns do you have about this technology?

What rules should organisations follow when creating personalised AI content?

Participants will be instructed not to include names, contact details, confidential information, or identifying information in their written responses.

3.6 Data Analysis Plan

Quantitative data will be exported into Microsoft Excel or an approved statistical analysis program.

The researcher will use descriptive statistics including:

Frequencies.
Percentages.
Means.
Tables.
Charts.
Comparison of average survey responses.

Where appropriate, correlation analysis may be used to examine relationships between:

AI knowledge and ability to identify AI content.
Personalisation and engagement.
Disclosure and trust.
Privacy concerns and willingness to provide data.
Previous AI experience and acceptance of AI-generated media.

Optional open-ended responses will be analysed using thematic analysis. Responses will be reviewed and grouped into themes such as:

Benefits of personalisation.
Privacy concerns.
Trust and authenticity.
Deepfake risks.
Voice cloning concerns.
AI transparency.
Copyright.
Accessibility.
Misinformation.
Need for regulation.

Any information that could identify a participant will be removed before analysis.

4. Ethical Considerations
4.1 Voluntary and Informed Participation

Before beginning the survey, each participant will receive a Participant Information Statement written in clear and accessible language.

The statement will explain:

The purpose of the research.
Why the person has been invited.
What participation involves.
The type of video or audio content that may be presented.
The approximate time required.
Potential risks and benefits.
How information will be collected and analysed.
How privacy and confidentiality will be protected.
The voluntary nature of participation.
The participant’s right to stop before submission.
The researcher’s and supervisor’s contact information.
How to make a complaint or raise a concern.

Participants will provide consent by selecting a statement such as:

I confirm that I am at least 18 years old. I have read and understood the Participant Information Statement. I understand that participation is voluntary, and I agree to participate in this research.

Participants who do not provide consent will not proceed to the questionnaire.

Participants may close the survey at any time before submission. No penalty will apply.

Because the survey is intended to be anonymous, it may not be possible to locate and remove an individual response after submission. This limitation will be clearly explained before consent is obtained.

4.2 Minimising Harm and Discomfort

The study is considered low risk. However, several potential risks have been identified.

Potential psychological discomfort

Some participants may feel uncomfortable when considering misinformation, deepfakes, privacy breaches, identity imitation, voice cloning, or unauthorised use of personal information.

To reduce this risk:

Participants may skip optional questions.
Participants may stop before submitting the survey.
Disturbing, offensive, discriminatory, or graphic content will not be used.
Survey questions will use neutral and respectful language.
Relevant student support or counselling information will be provided where appropriate.
Risk of deception

AI-generated video and audio may appear realistic and could be mistaken for authentic content.

To manage this risk:

Participants will be informed that the research concerns AI-generated media.
AI-generated samples will be clearly explained during or immediately after the relevant activity.
A debriefing statement will explain which examples were AI-generated.
No false claims will be made about real individuals.
The study will not publish misleading AI content outside the research setting.
Voice and identity risks

AI-generated audio may imitate a recognisable person’s voice, while AI-generated video may reproduce an individual’s face, appearance, or movements.

To manage this risk:

No participant’s face or voice will be cloned.
No real person’s identity will be reproduced without explicit written consent.
Generic, licensed, researcher-created, or authorised synthetic content will be used.
Participants will not be asked to upload facial images, voice recordings, or biometric information.
Misinformation and deepfake risks

Generative audio research has recognised the potential for fraud, deepfakes, impersonation, and copyright infringement.

The project will therefore not create content that:

Impersonates a real person without permission.
Requests money or private account information.
Makes false statements about a real individual.
Falsely represents political, medical, legal, or financial advice.
Could reasonably damage a person’s reputation.
Could be used to bypass identity verification.
Copyright risks

All video, audio, images, scripts, and music used in the project will be:

Created by the research group.
Used under an appropriate licence.
Obtained from authorised sources.
Used with written permission.
Properly acknowledged where required.

Copyrighted voices, songs, videos, or images will not be reproduced without permission.

4.3 Protecting Confidentiality and Privacy

The research will collect only the minimum amount of information required to answer the research questions.

Participants will not be asked to provide:

Full names.
Student identification numbers.
Residential addresses.
Personal telephone numbers.
Exact dates of birth.
Private account details.
Passwords.
Facial recognition data.
Voiceprints.
Personal photographs.
Private video or audio recordings.

The online survey will be configured not to collect identifying information wherever technically possible.

If email addresses are required to distribute the survey, they will not be included in the analysis dataset.

Responses will be assigned numerical codes, such as Participant 01 or Participant 02. Results will be reported as combined statistics or de-identified themes.

If a participant accidentally includes identifying information in an open-ended answer, the researcher will remove or redact the information before analysis.

No raw participant data will be uploaded to GitHub. Only non-sensitive project contributions, blank research materials, de-identified code, or screenshots without participant information may be uploaded for the Week 10 class contribution requirement. The original task requires each group member to submit evidence of an individual contribution.

4.4 Ensuring Fair and Respectful Treatment

All eligible participants will be treated fairly regardless of:

Gender.
Age group.
Cultural background.
Nationality.
Religion.
Disability.
Education level.
Technical experience.
Level of AI knowledge.

The questionnaire will use inclusive, accessible, and non-judgmental language.

The researcher will not assume that participants with limited knowledge of AI are less intelligent or less capable. Participants will not be judged based on whether they can correctly identify AI-generated content.

The research will also recognise that AI systems may contain bias because they are trained using existing datasets. The researcher will consider whether examples unfairly represent or disadvantage particular cultural, linguistic, or social groups.

Responsible AI research should identify and manage AI-related biases, explain the use and limitations of AI in language understandable to non-experts, and clearly label synthetic data or material.

4.5 Honesty with Professional Colleagues

The research team will report the study honestly and transparently.

The researchers will not:

Fabricate participant responses.
Change data to support a preferred result.
Remove negative findings without explanation.
Present AI-generated information as participant data.
Claim that correlation proves causation.
Conceal the use of generative AI.
Misrepresent another group member’s contribution.
Use AI as an author of the research.

The final report will explain:

How AI tools were used.
Which materials were AI-generated.
How participants were recruited.
The sample size.
The questionnaire design.
The data analysis methods.
The limitations of the research.
Potential bias in the sample.
Any incomplete or excluded responses.

Contributions from group members, participants, the supervisor, and academic sources will be appropriately acknowledged.

5. Data Management
5.1 Data Storage

Research data will be stored in a password-protected institutional account or another storage system approved by the supervisor.

Stored research records may include:

Anonymous questionnaire responses.
De-identified datasets.
Data analysis spreadsheets.
Approved AI-generated video and audio samples.
Participant information documents.
Blank consent documents.
Research notes.
The final research report.

Raw survey data will not be stored in a public repository.

5.2 Data Security

The following security measures will be used:

Password protection.
Multi-factor authentication where available.
Access limited to authorised research group members and the supervisor.
Encryption or appropriate institutional security controls.
Regular security updates for devices used in the research.
Removal of identifying information before analysis.
No public links to participant datasets.
No transfer of participant information through unsecured personal messaging services.
Review of screenshots before submission to ensure that participant data is not visible.
Secure backup storage approved by the institution.

If an external AI system is used, confidential participant information will not be entered into it. Survey responses, personal data, voice samples, facial images, or identifying details will not be uploaded to public generative AI platforms.

5.3 Data Retention

The research data will be retained for the period required by the institution’s research data policy or according to written instructions provided by the project supervisor.

If a specific period is not provided for this class project, the researchers will obtain guidance from the supervisor before deleting the data.

5.4 Data Disposal

At the end of the approved retention period:

Electronic datasets will be securely deleted.
Temporary downloaded copies will be removed.
Cloud-based working copies will be deleted where permitted.
Backup copies under the researchers’ control will be removed.
Video and audio samples containing restricted material will be securely deleted.
Paper documents containing confidential information will be cross-shredded or placed in an approved confidential disposal service.

Only the final de-identified academic report may be retained where permitted by institutional requirements.

